  "preamble": "Lateralization of the alveolar tap [ɾ] to [l]. In Caribbean Spanish (Puerto Rico, eastern and central Cuba, eastern Dominican Republic and Santo Domingo), the syllable-final tap often surfaces as [l]. In Polynesian, Proto-Polynesian *r (a tap) merged with *l in Samoic-Outlier languages and in Hawaiian, while Tahitian and Māori keep r. In western Cuba, where coda /ɾ/ and /l/ merge and assimilate to the next consonant, the tap also becomes [l] before /l/, as part of a total assimilation: burla bu[ll]a (Arias 2019).",
  "phoneticEffects": "Lateralization, Liquid Merger, Total Assimilation, Gemination",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Puerto Rican Spanish",
      "languageFamily": "Ibero-Romance",
      "examples": [
